{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 40,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get started, we wanted \nto let you know there’s some new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2600,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "limited edition Merch for sale at the Merch store."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5760,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "North America was recently graced with a \ntotal solar eclipse.Those of us lucky or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11000,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "obsessed enough to be on the narrow path \nof totality and … also lucky enough to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15840,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not be under a giant blanket of clouds \n… witnessed this most wondrous of all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19320,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "celestial phenomena. The Sun went dark, the \nsolar corona blazed, and even the ruby-red  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25040,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "top of the solar atmosphere became visible \nto the naked eye. Jupiter, Saturn, Venus,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even a comet straddled the new black-hole \nSun. Part of the reason the total solar eclipse  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36160,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is special is that it’s so rare. If it happened \nevery day you probably wouldn’t even look up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42720,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, good news for those of us who missed the \neclipse, whether due to weather or geography  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47360,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there is such a phenomenon. It’s that enormous \nball of rock that’s just hanging in our sky. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one that happens to be the exact size to blot \nout the Sun now and then, and without which we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may never have figured out how the universe \nworks—or begun our exploration of it. It’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65720,
    "dDurationMs": 13789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Moon, and it’s our stepping-stone to the \nuniverse. Today we learn how to take that step."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79509,
    "dDurationMs": 11,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "INTRO MUSIC"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79520,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Seriously, our Moon is crazy. You know those over \nthe top science fiction sky-scapes—multiple suns,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiple moons, gigantic moons or Saturn-like \nplanets or crazy nebulae that fill half the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91480,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sky. The reason we have to come up with that \nstuff is because our own sky is already pretty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sci-fi over-the-top. No other planet in our \nsolar system has anything like the Moon. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only physically larger natural satellite are \n3 of Jupiter’s and one of Saturn’s moons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and our Moon is still comparable to those in \nraw size. But the gas-giant moons are tiny  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112880,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compared to their host planet. Our Moon is \nby a long, long way the largest relative to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the planet it orbits, making the Earth and \nMoon practically a binary planet system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124600,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s generally believed that our moon formed when \na Mars-ish-sized planet that we call Theia slammed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129880,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the still-forming Earth, throwing up one of \nthose sci-fi planetary rings that quickly pulled  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134040,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself together into the Moon. And we’ve talked \nabout this hypothesis before. That seems like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140160,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a rare enough event that some argue that such \noutsized moons are probably rare in the cosmos."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146800,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If so, then an alien looking up at our \nsky would be like what the hell is that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing? And then they might think that it’s no \nwonder these otherwise primitive humans were so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158120,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quick to figure out so much about the universe \nfrom the big bang to the end of time. I mean,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162120,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we could only pretend that we are at \nthe center of a small universe for so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "long when we can literally see the detailed \nsurface of another world with the naked eye."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Moon is both our metaphorical and literal \nstepping stone to the universe. I’ll come back  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177520,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the literal part in a minute—let me \njust drive in the metaphorical part a bit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "harder. The Moon gives us a few telling \nclues about our place in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188680,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1) It's round. The moon is pretty clearly a \ngiant ball of rock with a cratered surface.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194080,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s a world. Surely an essential clue \nthat the other bodies of the solar system,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198600,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "including the Earth, are of similar form."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202360,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2) Its eclipses and lunar phases. We saw this \nlast episode. These phenomena told us the basic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209160,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "structure of the Earth-Moon-Sun system, but \nalso allowed us to figure out the distances  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213520,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the Sun and Moon—our first hard calibration \nof the immensity of space. And watching the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220680,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s round shadow cross the Moon really \ndrilled in the whole Earth isn’t flat thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3) The Moon's gravitational attraction to \nEarth. It’s said that Isaac Newton figured  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out his law of universal gravitation \nafter watching an apple fall. But the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237360,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other part of the story is that to figure \nout the relationship between gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241200,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force and distance he needed a much more \ndistant object that is also subject to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246120,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s gravity. He used the Moon. Without the \nMoon it may have taken much longer to figure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out gravity. And without that don’t even \nthink about starting up a space program."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257000,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the Moon has been one of the most important \ntheoretical stepping stones to our understanding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261640,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the universe. And we’ve long understood \nthat it could also be our literal stepping stone:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267520,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is humanity’s first destination beyond \nour atmosphere. Through the Apollo programs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273640,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "humans walked on another world. The last \ntime that happened was over half a century  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ago. It’s no longer something we do; it’s \nsomething we did—like, back in history."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285280,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s been argued that we no longer need to \nput people on the Moon. We got all the rocks,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290880,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that particular cold war ended. And perhaps \nmost importantly we convinced ourselves that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295360,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inter-world travel is possible. Our \neyes became fixed on Mars as the next  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299960,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "human destination. But can you imagine us \neven contemplating a trip to Mars without  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "having first landed on the Moon? Thanks Moon—job \nwell done. Please continue your regular duties  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of sloshing our oceans and making the \nnight a bit more romantic and moon-lit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317280,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except, are we really done with the Moon? \nIn recent years our collective intentions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323800,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have swiveled back to the great sky \nrock. Just last month, the very first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "private company achieved a soft landing \non the Moon. Intuitive Machines managed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334880,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to touch down its Odyseus lander about \n300km from the lunar south pole and took  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340200,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spectacular images of the surface and gathered \nimportant data for future missions. The soft  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345680,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "landing could’ve been softer because \nOdysseus tipped over on touch-down,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which did shorten its life expectancy but didn’t \ndent its overall success. This was such a major  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356360,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "achievement because the lunar poles are \nmore heavily cratered than the equator,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361040,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and experience extreme temperature fluctuations \nthat landers have to be resilient against."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367000,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Intuitive Machines didn’t just achieve \nthe first private softish landing;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371640,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this was the first soft landing by the \nUnited States in the 52 years since Apollo  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376480,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "17. But our return to the Moon has been \nbuilding up momentum for some years now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382200,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In August last year India became only the 4th \nnation to soft-land on the Moon. Its Vikram  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389200,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lander, part of the Chandrayaan-3 mission, was \nalso the first craft to land in the lunar south  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pole region where it successfully deployed \nits Pragyan rover to explore the area.. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399280,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But why this current obsession with the lunar \nsouth pole, if the poles are so hazardous? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405720,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because of a game-changing fact that India’s first \nmoon mission—-Chandrayaan-1—helped establish. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412160,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "found water at the south pole. Back in 2008, \nChandrayaan-1 dropped its Moon Impact Probe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419960,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the Shackleton Crater. The “hard-landing” \ncraft detected significant water in the tenuous  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426920,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "south pole atmosphere its descent, confirming \nearlier evidence from a number of other missions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To be clear, these detections are not quite \ndirect, and require interpretation. There’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439120,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strong evidence for hydrogen in the tenuous \nlunar atmosphere, likely from actual ice or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445400,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water or hydroxil bearing minerals in the soil, \nand direct detection of those minerals. We’ve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452720,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also known for a while that there was real \nwater ice in the permanent shadows of some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457120,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lunar craters. But there’s an emerging consensus \nthat there must be significant ice even in sunlit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464480,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lunar soil. That means there’s just way more \nwater than we thought. It’s not even clear  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how it got there. Perhaps it was brought in \nby comets, just like Earth’s oceans might  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476120,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have been. Or perhaps protons in the solar \nwind slam into oxygen in lunar minerals to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482280,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produce H2O. There’s some evidence that that lunar \nsurface water is being continuously replenished,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the latter may well be important. Whatever \nthe source, the presence of water turns the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494960,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moon from a pretty but dead sky rock \ninto our stepping stone to the stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502360,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you’ve spent any time in Arakis you \nknow why water is such a game changer.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508560,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although it’s abundant on Earth and \nCaladan, it’s heavy and so expensive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to pull out of a gravity well. It costs a \ncouple of thousand US dollars to get every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "litre of water to the international space \nstation, let alone to the moon. Even with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fremen Stim-suit level water recycling, \ngood luck building a decent sized base,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532840,
    "dDurationMs": 9920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let alone even a small biosphere. But an \nenormous lunar source makes all of this possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542760,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which brings us back to human plans for the Moon, \nand to China. China has been vigorously pursuing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549840,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lunar exploration for longer than India with its \nuncrewed Chang’e program. Chang’e-3’s successful  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557360,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2013 landing made China the 3rd nation to achieve \na soft landing after the US and Russia efforts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563680,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both ended in the 70s. Chang’e-4 was the first \nanything to land on the far side of the Moon in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570800,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2019, and Chang’e-5 the first non-crewed lander \nto successfully return lunar material to Earth.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578120,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That was in 2020. The next Chang’e - number 6 - \nis supposed to launch next month and to return  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584280,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "samples from the apparently watery south pole. \nNumber 7 will also mess around at the south pole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590240,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but number 8 … Chang’e-8 will do a 3-D \nprinting experiment using lunar regolith  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596120,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and will carry a sealed ecosystem experiment. \nAll in preparation for the next phase—a crewed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602320,
    "dDurationMs": 8520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lunar base. China aims to land humans on the \nMoon by 2030 and by 2035-ish to establish a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610840,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "permanent base in collaboration with Russia, \nbut ostensibly as an international facility."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617520,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The United States may have been the first, and \nstill the only nation to put a human on the Moon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but has been distracted by another shiny \nthing in the sky. NASA’s space exploration  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629560,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has really been focused on Mars—and with \nexcellent reason. Besides being the next  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634480,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "obvious human destination after the Moon, we’ve \nknown for much longer that Mars has water—both  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639320,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to support a base but also perhaps to once \nhave supported life. The impact of finding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644120,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evidence of that would be much, much bigger than \neven the first Moon landing, in my opinion. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650760,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Moon could never have evolved life, but with \nwater it could support our life in the future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657560,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so again the United States is finally \nturning its eyes back to the Moon. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Artemis program aims to send crewed missions \non the Moon—first in orbit and then to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666880,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface. It consists of the Space Launch \nSystem—a super-heavy lift rocket—carrying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672040,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Orion spacecraft. Other vehicles will \nalso serve the larger Artemis mission,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676720,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the SpaceX Falcon heavy carrying the Dragon \nspacecraft. In 2022 the un-crewed Artemis 1  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mission put an Orion spacecraft into lunar \norbit before successfully returning it to Earth.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689920,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2025 Artemis 2 will do the same, \nbut with people—four astronauts. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696240,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then in 2026 Artemis 3 aims to put the first \nAmerican boots on lunar soil since Apollo 17."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702880,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other big goal of the Artemis program is the \nconstruction of the Lunar Gateway space station—a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crewed station that will be constructed in lunar \norbit over the rest of this decade. This one is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715120,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with participation of the Canadian, Japanese, \nEuropean, and Dubai space agencies. There has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721040,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been some criticism of Gateway—basically amounting \nto the question of why build a station in orbit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726120,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when you could build a station on the Moon. \nFair point, but ultimately the US is planning  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730120,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to build a surface base, and the Gateway \nstation is supposed to support that goal,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as well as be a general staging station \nfor a variety of solar system missions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740800,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Speaking of, there’s something else that \nlunar water is good for. Apply an electric  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745080,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "current to H2O and the molecule splits giving \nyou hydrogen and oxygen. This is electrolysis.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751760,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These gases very readily recombine into \nH2O, releasing the same energy it took to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "separate them—but now explosively. Like, in \na rocket. Water can be used to make rocket  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fuel. The idea would be to place some giant \nsolar arrays on the surface near the poles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767880,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and use the energy to slowly harvest hydrogen and \noxygen, turning the Moon into a refueling depot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775360,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The weight of fuel is one of the biggest \nlimiting factors in determining spacecraft weight  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and range. And dragging that fuel out of Earth’s \nprodigious gravitational well is the hardest part.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785840,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With a lunar fuel depot, the most fuel any mission \nneeds to carry is the amount needed to reach the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791000,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Moon. From there it can be refueled for its onward \njourney to Mars, the asteroid belt mines, its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "return to Earth, or perhaps to some destination \nbeyond the solar system. And sure, I guess being  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "able to dock at the Lunar Gateway is better than \nhaving to land on the Moon before moving on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806640,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If all goes according to the various plans, \nin as little as 10 years we’ll have our first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812760,
    "dDurationMs": 8440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deep-space space station, our first crewed lunar \nbase. Maybe a couple of them. And we’ll be working  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821200,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "towards the ability to refuel spacecraft from \nlunar orbit. There are plenty of other plans  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826280,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also—like the giant radio telescope on the \nfar side of the Moon that we talked about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829760,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "previously. For the most part our return to the \nMoon seems like a positive thing, with lots of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "international collaboration to build a shared \nand peace-forward infrastructure. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842600,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the US just announced a move to create an \ninternationally agreed Lunar Standard Time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to account for the fact that time ticks a tiny bit \nslower on the Moon compared to Earth’s surface on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852880,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "account of general relativity. But there are \nsigns of competition already arising, and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859080,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whiff of a new space race in the air. China versus \nthe US seems to be the main superpower standoff,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866440,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with each asserting their leadership. Russia is \naligning more with China, Europe with the US,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871920,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and various nations sidling in one direction \nor the other. And then there’s the private  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877960,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sector which will play increasingly large, if \nnot dominant part of the game going forward."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883920,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whatever the outcome, it’s coming, and pretty \nfast. Odds are that in the decade—perhaps much  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quicker—somone will be back on the Moon \nor close to it. And probably that someone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894680,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will be human, which is a win for all of us. \nWe’ve been snoozing a bit on the Moon, but soon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901920,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ll be walking on it. Again. And hopefully soon \nafter, that weirdly giant sky rock will fulfil its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908840,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "potential as a fortuitous stepping stone towards \nour future exploration of the rest of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915960,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everybody. Before we go we wanted to let \nyou know we have two new limited edition Merch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921040,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "items up at the Merch store. First up is our \nfirst ever Black Hole Enamel pin. This is a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927240,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1.5 inch enamel pin on 2x3 inch holo foil \nbacking card designed by the Space Time gfx  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933200,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "team. These pins are limited edition pre-sale and \nyou will need to put your order in by April 29th  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the pins shipping during the second week of \nJune. Once we’re out of stock from the pre-sale,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s it. Next up is our Star Wars inspired \nSpace Time t-shirt and sweatshirt. Designed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950840,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by our very star wars nerdy director, you can now \nexpress your love and knowledge of the force. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954040,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "limited edition design will be available until May \nThe 4th. And if you’d like your shirt to arrive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before Star Wars day, you’ll need to put your \norder in by April 25th. To put your order in,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967400,
    "dDurationMs": 21520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just head over pbsspacetime.com/shop \nor click the link the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
